course: dictionary
lesson: 1059
title: Both: two together
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: «Both» means the two of them together, not just one: «Both hands are needed to lift the box.» Use it when you are talking about exactly two things.
explain: «Both … and» joins two things that are true at the same time: «She is both tall and strong.» Both parts of the sentence have to be true. If one part is false, the joining is wrong.
explain: Other speakers use «both» too. «Yous both can come to the park» is casual talk from Ireland and Australia, and it means «you two». Writing for a formal exam might say «each of the two» or «jointly», but «both» is the everyday word.
fact: Both means the two of them together, never just one.
fact: «Both» can come before a noun, as in «both hands», or before «of», as in «both of the cats».
fact: «Both … and» joins two things that are true together, as in «both tall and strong».
fact: In Ireland and Australia you may hear «yous both», which means «you two».
fact: Formal writing may say «each of the two» or «jointly» instead of «both».
exercise: «Pair the numbers from both ends to add fast: 1 + 4 = 5 and 2 + 3 = 5.» What does both mean here? | *The two ends together | Only the first end | Nothing at all | Every number in the sum
exercise: Ella says «both» means only one thing. Is she right? | Yes, it means the whole class | No, it means nothing at all | Yes, it means only one thing | *No, both means the two of them together
exercise: Which word starts the pair in «He can both sing and dance»? | *both | sing | dance | and
test: «The monkey eats with both hands.» How many hands does it use at once? | *Two | One | Three | None
test: In casual Irish talk, «yous both» means: | the whole school | me and my dog | *you two | everyone who has ever lived
exam: In formal writing, which phrase means the same as «both» for two pupils? | nobody at all | *each of the two | every one of the twenty | a single one
